// proof_patterns: trust travels the FULL closure, not just one hop.
//
// A CONTROL for monotone trust propagation. `composition_trusted_helper` proves the one-hop case:
// a proved function that calls a trusted one states the assumption. It cannot show whether the
// assumption survives an intermediate hop, and that is the property the authority transition
// actually requires — "trust and assumptions propagate monotonically through the FULL closure".
//
// Shape: outer -> middle -> leaf, with `leaf` trusted. `middle` is proved and assumes `leaf`.
// `outer` never mentions `leaf` and must still report the assumption, because a claim that dropped
// it two hops from the boundary would launder exactly what the one-hop qualification prevents: a
// reader sees a kernel-checked `outer` and cannot tell part of the chain was never proved.
//
// Expected: BOTH `middle` and `outer` report proved AND state that they assume `calls.leaf`.
mod calls {
    // Audited boundary, deliberately unproved — the same shape as `composition_trusted_helper`'s.
    trusted fn leaf(x: i32) -> i32 {
        return x * 2;
    }

    // One hop from the boundary.
    fn middle(x: i32) -> i32 {
        return leaf(x);
    }

    // TWO hops. Its own body mentions no trusted function at all.
    fn outer(x: i32) -> i32 {
        return middle(x) + 1;
    }
}
